WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.917
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Are
you migrating to Cloud?

00:00:01.917 --> 00:00:03.510
What should you migrate first?

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:04.650
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:08.059
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:16.520
Welcome to Cloud Migration 101,
where we share migration tips

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:17.060
and tricks.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:17.622
I'm Priyanka.

00:00:17.622 --> 00:00:18.830
KYLE MOSCHETTO: And I'm Kyle.

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:20.450
And in this episode,
we'll walk you

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:22.670
through the process of
choosing the first application

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:24.230
to migrate to Google Cloud.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:25.897
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Let's say, today, I'm

00:00:25.897 --> 00:00:28.520
the head of engineering at
Clow D Corp. We make automated

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.200
floodgates for water mills.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.000
As you can imagine,
our infrastructure

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.970
is complicated with a large
number of applications,

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:37.555
and I'm not sure which
application to move first.

00:00:37.555 --> 00:00:39.930
KYLE MOSCHETTO: Well, let me
help demystify this process.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.920
Let's start by seeing all of
your apps and what they are.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.365
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yes,
so here are all our apps.

00:00:45.365 --> 00:00:47.073
KYLE MOSCHETTO: Ah,
the first thing to do

00:00:47.073 --> 00:00:49.220
is to group your
applications into categories.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:51.470
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: All of
my single-tier applications,

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:54.200
multi-tier applications,
databases, and dev tools

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:55.610
are grouped together now.

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:56.610
KYLE MOSCHETTO: Awesome.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:59.180
Now we find one common example
in each category, something

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:00.680
that does a good
job of representing

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:01.610
the entire category.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:03.110
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
For this, we will

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:06.410
have to drill deeper into what
makes an app a good candidate

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:08.360
for being the first to migrate.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:09.380
KYLE MOSCHETTO: Exactly.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.930
And there's a list of criteria
that I generally recommend.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:13.410
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: All right,
let's go through this list

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:13.880
then.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:15.320
KYLE MOSCHETTO: A good
first application to migrate

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:16.940
should be something that's
typical of your existing

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.003
workloads but nothing
that's business-critical.

00:01:19.003 --> 00:01:21.170
We want to use this application
to build a knowledge

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:23.960
base within our organization
around how to migrate

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.202
applications to the cloud.

00:01:25.202 --> 00:01:26.660
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
For this reason,

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:29.720
we should not pick an app that
is extremely business-critical,

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.480
but we should also not
pick an app that is used,

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:34.160
say, a few times a year.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.980
Try to find a balance of
moderately business-critical

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:40.923
application so it helps increase
the momentum of the migration.

00:01:40.923 --> 00:01:42.590
KYLE MOSCHETTO: Now,
the second criteria

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:44.132
is that we want an
application that's

00:01:44.132 --> 00:01:46.220
managed by a central
team so it has support

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:49.208
and, ideally, has an application
owner who embraces innovation.

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:51.500
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Picking
a high-performance team that

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.470
sits in the main office with a
proven history of implementing

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:57.560
modern dev practices,
DevOps, and SRE

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.000
can be a good candidate.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.940
If these apps have leadership
sponsors and clear goals,

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:02.985
that would be perfect.

00:02:02.985 --> 00:02:04.610
KYLE MOSCHETTO: Now,
the third criteria

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:06.610
is that we want to try
and find something that's

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:09.720
relatively standalone with a
low number of network security

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.720
and application
dependencies and, also,

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.250
doesn't need any
code refactoring

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:14.210
to move to the cloud.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:16.252
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Stateless
apps would be great,

00:02:16.252 --> 00:02:19.220
because they don't require any
data migration, or an app that

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:21.835
only needs configuration
changes, but no code changes.

00:02:21.835 --> 00:02:23.960
KYLE MOSCHETTO: Next, the
data storage requirements

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:25.115
should be relatively small.

00:02:25.115 --> 00:02:26.990
If your application has
petabytes of storage,

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:28.970
you probably shouldn't
use that as a first mover.

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:29.600
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
This is mainly

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.120
because we don't want to
start off with an edge case

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.280
by picking an app that
is not representative

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:35.622
of most of the other apps.

00:02:35.622 --> 00:02:37.580
KYLE MOSCHETTO: The
application should also not

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.400
be subjected to any complicated
compliance or licensing

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:41.090
challenges.

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:43.548
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Make sure
the licensing terms are clear.

00:02:43.548 --> 00:02:46.460
Check if the license allows
you to run the app in the cloud

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:47.240
or not.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:49.590
You may also have specific
compliance requirements.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.560
For example, in some
countries, the customer

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.753
has the right to decide
where to store their data.

00:02:53.753 --> 00:02:55.170
KYLE MOSCHETTO:
For these reasons,

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:57.470
we recommend such apps
not be the first ones

00:02:57.470 --> 00:02:58.370
that you migrate.

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:00.590
And last, but not least,
we want an application that

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.150
can afford a cutover window.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:05.010
If the application has to
stay up 100% of the time,

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:07.850
it is not a great candidate for
your first mover application.

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:09.920
Your teams are going to be
learning with the first mover,

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.212
and we want to be able to
provide them a cushion, where

00:03:12.212 --> 00:03:15.140
if they make a mistake, it's not
detrimental to your business.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:16.807
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Since an application,

00:03:16.807 --> 00:03:19.070
like batch processing, can
tolerate a longer downtime

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:20.960
than the
customer-facing apps, it

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:22.730
makes sense to pick
batch processing

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:24.800
service over the
e-commerce apps,

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:26.420
assuming it meets
other criteria,

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:27.490
as we discussed earlier.

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:28.490
KYLE MOSCHETTO: Exactly.

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:29.865
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Now, let's try

00:03:29.865 --> 00:03:32.150
and apply this to our
applications at Clow D Corp.

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:33.920
So here's a landscape
of applications.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:36.925
We already grouped the
apps based on categories.

00:03:36.925 --> 00:03:38.300
KYLE MOSCHETTO:
Among these apps,

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:40.460
what are some of your
high-performance teams?

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:43.160
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Well,
inventory, payment, SaaS

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:46.880
and mobile apps, document
database, SQL database,

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:49.395
and CI tools are some of
our high-performance apps.

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:51.020
KYLE MOSCHETTO: And
out of those, which

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:53.510
would require
refactoring or migration

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:56.013
has major dependencies
that it would require?

00:03:56.013 --> 00:03:57.680
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
SaaS and mobile apps,

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.960
along with SQL database,
have dependencies

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:03.390
and may require refactoring, but
others are fairly independent.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:05.720
KYLE MOSCHETTO: OK, what
about serious edge cases?

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:08.300
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: SQL
database is huge and also won't

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:11.540
lead to a ton of learning
that can apply to other apps,

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:15.260
except for, maybe, another
SQL database that is internal,

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:16.953
so that could be
a later migration.

00:04:16.953 --> 00:04:17.870
KYLE MOSCHETTO: Great.

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:20.390
Do any of these have
licensing concerns?

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:23.180
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: No, but there
are some data compliance things

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:27.050
that we need to take care of
around users and data in EU

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:30.398
for our SaaS application, so
we cannot start with that.

00:04:30.398 --> 00:04:31.190
KYLE MOSCHETTO: OK.

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:33.770
And which of these apps can
afford a decent cutover window?

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:35.812
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: CI
tools and inventory service

00:04:35.812 --> 00:04:37.960
can afford a cutover
window, for sure.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:39.980
Payment service and
SQL database has

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:42.480
to be 100% available
all the time,

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.783
so those will be a no-go
for the first phase.

00:04:45.783 --> 00:04:48.200
KYLE MOSCHETTO: Well, with
that, you have the list of apps

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:49.492
that you want to migrate first.

00:04:49.492 --> 00:04:50.760
Start with the inventory app.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:51.680
Learn from that.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:54.740
Apply it to the mobile app,
the CI tool, the SQL database,

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:55.910
and do that in sequence.

00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:58.035
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Remember,
it's really beneficial

00:04:58.035 --> 00:04:58.940
to start small.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:02.090
Move a very simple application
first, and learn a bunch

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:05.060
about how you're going to run
your larger and more complex

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:06.448
applications on Google Cloud.

00:05:06.448 --> 00:05:08.240
KYLE MOSCHETTO: And by
using this approach,

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.610
you'll set your business up
for a successful migration

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:11.565
to Google Cloud.

00:05:11.565 --> 00:05:13.190
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
So today, we learned

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:15.680
how to decide what
to migrate first.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:18.050
We started by grouping
our apps into categories

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:20.090
and then apply these
different categories

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:21.900
to make the right choice.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:23.690
If you are in the
process of migration,

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:25.370
don't forget to
like and subscribe,

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:28.820
because we don't want you to
miss any episode of this Cloud

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:30.380
Migration 101 series.

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:33.730
[MUSIC PLAYING]

